1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,740
A wet garden path after the rain.

2
00:00:02,740 --> 00:00:04,407
An earthworm crawls out.

3
00:00:04,808 --> 00:00:08,125
Its body is a long chain of
rings, all looking alike.

4
00:00:08,575 --> 00:00:11,027
Diya watched it stretch and squeeze along.

5
00:00:11,027 --> 00:00:15,638
She wants to be a doctor, so she asked:
are those rings only on the outside?

6
00:00:15,638 --> 00:00:18,647
And what is inside, between
the skin and the gut?

7
00:00:18,997 --> 00:00:20,769
Two features answer that.

8
00:00:20,769 --> 00:00:22,755
The coelom, a body cavity.

9
00:00:22,755 --> 00:00:25,301
And segmentation, the repeated rings.

10
00:00:25,801 --> 00:00:30,423
By the end, you will know coelomates,
pseudocoelomates and acoelomates,

11
00:00:30,423 --> 00:00:33,946
what metamerism means, and
where the notochord fits.

12
00:00:34,596 --> 00:00:38,533
First, picture an animal
body as a tube inside a tube.

13
00:00:38,883 --> 00:00:41,058
The outer tube is the body wall.

14
00:00:41,058 --> 00:00:42,889
The inner tube is the gut.

15
00:00:43,339 --> 00:00:46,542
The key question: is there a
space between the two tubes?

16
00:00:46,542 --> 00:00:48,467
And if there is, what lines it?

17
00:00:49,117 --> 00:00:50,924
Let's zoom into the earthworm.

18
00:00:51,274 --> 00:00:55,622
From outside, the body is divided
into segments, one after another, from

19
00:00:55,622 --> 00:00:57,400
the front end to the back.

20
00:00:57,900 --> 00:01:00,927
Zoom in and cut it open along its length.

21
00:01:00,927 --> 00:01:06,076
Thin walls divide the inside too, and
some organs repeat in every segment.

22
00:01:06,576 --> 00:01:08,465
Now cut straight across the body.

23
00:01:08,465 --> 00:01:11,791
Here is the body wall, and
here is the gut in the middle.

24
00:01:12,291 --> 00:01:16,846
And between them is a fluid-filled
space, lined all around by mesoderm,

25
00:01:16,846 --> 00:01:18,466
the middle germ layer.

26
00:01:18,466 --> 00:01:20,300
That space is the coelom.

27
00:01:20,950 --> 00:01:24,287
Now let's sort animals by
that space, step by step.

28
00:01:24,637 --> 00:01:25,537
Step one.

29
00:01:25,466 --> 00:01:29,104
The body cavity lined by
mesoderm is called the coelom.

30
00:01:29,104 --> 00:01:31,539
Animals that have it are coelomates.

31
00:01:32,289 --> 00:01:37,396
Examples: annelids, molluscs, arthropods,
echinoderms, hemichordates and

32
00:01:37,396 --> 00:01:38,301
chordates.

33
00:01:39,051 --> 00:01:39,951
Step two.

34
00:01:39,934 --> 00:01:43,811
In some animals, the cavity
is not lined by mesoderm.

35
00:01:43,811 --> 00:01:48,518
The mesoderm sits only as scattered
pouches between the ectoderm and the

36
00:01:48,518 --> 00:01:49,418
endoderm.

37
00:01:50,086 --> 00:01:53,300
Such a cavity is a
pseudocoelom, a false coelom.

38
00:01:53,300 --> 00:01:55,488
These animals are pseudocoelomates.

39
00:01:55,488 --> 00:01:58,321
Example: the aschelminthes,
the roundworms.

40
00:01:59,071 --> 00:02:00,056
Step three.

41
00:02:00,056 --> 00:02:02,979
Some animals have no body cavity at all.

42
00:02:02,979 --> 00:02:04,563
They are acoelomates.

43
00:02:04,563 --> 00:02:05,880
A means without.

44
00:02:05,880 --> 00:02:09,039
Example: the platyhelminthes,
the flatworms.

45
00:02:09,789 --> 00:02:10,954
Remember this.

46
00:02:10,954 --> 00:02:14,546
A true coelom is a body
cavity lined by mesoderm.

47
00:02:15,146 --> 00:02:17,598
Now the second feature: segmentation.

48
00:02:17,948 --> 00:02:22,407
In some animals, the body is divided
into segments outside and inside,

49
00:02:22,407 --> 00:02:25,588
and at least some organs
repeat in each segment.

50
00:02:26,038 --> 00:02:30,839
This pattern is called metameric
segmentation, and the phenomenon is called

51
00:02:30,839 --> 00:02:31,745
metamerism.

52
00:02:31,745 --> 00:02:33,734
Part after part, repeated.

53
00:02:34,184 --> 00:02:38,482
In the earthworm, waves of muscle
squeeze along the segments, one after

54
00:02:38,482 --> 00:02:39,382
another.

55
00:02:39,157 --> 00:02:40,726
That is how it crawls.

56
00:02:41,376 --> 00:02:44,831
One more feature from this part
of the chapter: the notochord.

57
00:02:45,181 --> 00:02:48,815
The notochord is a rod-like
structure, made from mesoderm.

58
00:02:48,815 --> 00:02:52,819
It forms on the dorsal side,
along the back, in the embryo.

59
00:02:53,269 --> 00:02:56,373
Animals that form a
notochord are chordates.

60
00:02:56,373 --> 00:03:01,468
Animals that never form it are
non-chordates, like porifera to echinoderms.

61
00:03:01,918 --> 00:03:05,715
So the earthworm, with all its
rings, is still a non-chordate.

62
00:03:05,715 --> 00:03:09,385
A fish embryo forms a notochord,
so the fish is a chordate.

63
00:03:10,035 --> 00:03:14,119
Now the hardest pair to tell apart:
a true coelom and a false one.

64
00:03:14,469 --> 00:03:15,672
The earthworm.

65
00:03:15,672 --> 00:03:18,912
Its cavity is lined by
mesoderm all around.

66
00:03:18,912 --> 00:03:20,183
A true coelom.

67
00:03:20,633 --> 00:03:22,394
The roundworm, Ascaris.

68
00:03:22,394 --> 00:03:27,028
Its cavity has mesoderm only in
scattered pouches, not as a lining.

69
00:03:27,028 --> 00:03:28,202
A pseudocoelom.

70
00:03:28,802 --> 00:03:29,735
Your turn.

71
00:03:29,735 --> 00:03:33,821
In a hookworm, the body cavity
is not lined by mesoderm.

72
00:03:33,821 --> 00:03:36,010
True coelom, or pseudocoelom?

73
00:03:36,010 --> 00:03:37,382
Pause and decide.

74
00:03:40,582 --> 00:03:41,499
Pseudocoelom!

75
00:03:41,499 --> 00:03:46,055
The hookworm is an aschelminth, a
roundworm, so it is a pseudocoelomate.

76
00:03:46,705 --> 00:03:48,459
Now, a worked example.

77
00:03:48,459 --> 00:03:49,881
Three cross-sections.

78
00:03:49,881 --> 00:03:51,106
Let's sort them.

79
00:03:51,456 --> 00:03:52,356
First.

80
00:03:52,099 --> 00:03:56,548
Body wall and gut, with tissue
packed in between and no cavity.

81
00:03:56,548 --> 00:03:57,455
Acoelomate.

82
00:03:57,455 --> 00:04:00,102
That fits a flatworm, like Fasciola.

83
00:04:00,552 --> 00:04:01,452
Second.

84
00:04:01,237 --> 00:04:05,129
A cavity, but the mesoderm
is only in scattered pouches.

85
00:04:05,129 --> 00:04:06,281
Pseudocoelomate.

86
00:04:06,281 --> 00:04:08,887
That fits a roundworm, like Ascaris.

87
00:04:09,337 --> 00:04:10,237
Third.

88
00:04:10,061 --> 00:04:12,743
A cavity fully lined by mesoderm.

89
00:04:12,743 --> 00:04:13,705
Coelomate.

90
00:04:14,155 --> 00:04:18,886
And if that body is also segmented,
with organs repeating in each segment,

91
00:04:18,886 --> 00:04:22,166
it fits an annelid, like
the earthworm Pheretima.

92
00:04:22,816 --> 00:04:24,282
Now, the exam trap.

93
00:04:24,632 --> 00:04:27,484
Many students call any
body cavity a coelom.

94
00:04:27,884 --> 00:04:30,618
But a coelom must be lined by mesoderm.

95
00:04:30,618 --> 00:04:35,657
A roundworm has a cavity, yet it is only
a pseudocoelom, because its mesoderm

96
00:04:35,657 --> 00:04:37,381
is in scattered pouches.

97
00:04:38,031 --> 00:04:39,532
Where is this asked?

98
00:04:39,532 --> 00:04:43,995
As statement questions that pair a
group with its body cavity, or with

99
00:04:43,995 --> 00:04:45,900
metamerism or the notochord.

100
00:04:46,500 --> 00:04:47,718
Exam level now.

101
00:04:47,718 --> 00:04:49,514
Read the four statements.

102
00:04:49,514 --> 00:04:51,207
Which ones are correct?

103
00:04:51,207 --> 00:04:52,476
Pause and solve.

104
00:04:57,876 --> 00:04:59,167
The answer is D.

105
00:04:59,167 --> 00:05:01,367
Statements one, three and four.

106
00:05:01,867 --> 00:05:02,976
Two is wrong.

107
00:05:02,976 --> 00:05:06,970
In aschelminthes, the mesoderm
is only in scattered pouches.

108
00:05:06,970 --> 00:05:09,127
Their cavity is a pseudocoelom.

109
00:05:09,627 --> 00:05:11,809
One, three and four are right.

110
00:05:11,809 --> 00:05:16,212
Flatworms have no cavity, the earthworm
is metameric, and the notochord

111
00:05:16,212 --> 00:05:18,858
is a mesodermal rod on the dorsal side.

112
00:05:19,458 --> 00:05:20,917
Here is your recap.

113
00:05:20,917 --> 00:05:22,417
Screenshot this card.

114
00:05:25,617 --> 00:05:28,454
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

115
00:05:28,854 --> 00:05:29,754
One.

116
00:05:29,376 --> 00:05:31,218
What lines a true coelom?

117
00:05:32,118 --> 00:05:33,018
Two.

118
00:05:32,705 --> 00:05:35,937
Name one pseudocoelomate
and one acoelomate.

119
00:05:36,837 --> 00:05:37,737
Three.

120
00:05:37,534 --> 00:05:39,109
What is metamerism?

121
00:05:40,209 --> 00:05:44,791
Next time: ten animals on a museum
shelf, from a sponge to a worm with

122
00:05:44,791 --> 00:05:45,691
a collar.

123
00:05:45,600 --> 00:05:47,482
Can one key sort them all?

124
00:05:47,482 --> 00:05:49,709
We find out in non-chordate phyla.
